## 3. What is done

### Engine understood from source
`STRATEGY.md` has the full derivation. The load-bearing findings:
- **Market-limited, not production-limited.** Each product has a revenue
  ceiling: MILK ~$46k @200u, WOOL ~$45k @200u, STRAWBERRY ~$60k @400u,
  MELON ~$33k @150u, FERTILIZER ~$24k @400u. EGG and WHEAT never crash
  (log decay) — EGG holds ~$40/unit even at 1600 units.
- **Animals produce fertilizer unconditionally**, every day, fed or not.
  Nobody in town consumes fertilizer, but 300-400 units still sells for
  ~$24k. It is free money for one COLLECT action.
- **CARE banks a bonus**: goose 1→2 eggs/day, cow 1→3 milk/2days,
  sheep 1→4 wool/3days. Full daily feed+care strictly beats rationing.
- **Optimal sale timing is late** (town drain is monotone, so inventory is
  lowest and price highest at season end) — bounded only by `shedCapacity=100`.
- Selling at the $1 floor does not add to market inventory.
- Atomic PLANT validation: if PLANT requests for one crop exceed seeds held
  in a turn, **all** of them are dropped.

### Agent (`agent/main.py`)
Runtime planner: fixed tile roles from a priority queue, one contiguous zone
per unit, per-tile checklist (build → place → plant → water → fertilize →
feed → care → collect → harvest), errand mechanic for shed round-trips,
market module for hire/buy/sell.

Implemented and verified:
- nearest-to-shed tile ordering
- workload-based hand sizing with a cash-proportional hiring budget
- animal build order (sheep before cow) — searchable
- endgame reserve-price ramp, score-relative risk multiplier
- **opponent supply prediction** (`_forecast_supply`, `_market_pressure`):
  reads the opponent's public farm, projects how many units of each product
  they will still dump this season, compares against remaining town demand,
  and lowers our reserve price when the market is forecast to be oversupplied
  (race to sell) or raises it when undersupplied (hold). Verified: 10 cows
  produce 150 milk over 10 days against 130 units of remaining demand →
  pressure 2.31 → correctly signals "sell now".

### Search infrastructure
`search/ga_search.py` — (mu+lambda) ES over ~28 hyperparameters including the
portfolio mix (`TC_COW/SHEEP/GOOSE/STRAWBERRY/MELON/WHEAT`). Evaluates on the
real engine, 26 workers. Opponent kinds supported in `evaluate.py`:
`"starter"` / `"random"` (builtins), `("self", params)`, `("replay", name)`,
`("ref", name)`.

### Reference agents (`opponents/`)
The user uploaded 8 public notebooks. `opponents/extract_agents.py` pulls the
agent source out of each (they use base64 or base85+zlib payloads, or a
`%%writefile main.py` cell) **without running their bootstrap cells**. Result:

| file | vs starter |
|---|---|
| `v111-8c4s-economic-core-premium-lead.py` | **121,478** |
| `kaggriculture-rank-your-agent.py` | 121,168 |
| `kaggriculture-3000-socre.py` | 119,293 |
| `kaggriculture-ttv1.py` | 119,293 (identical to 3000-socre) |
| `kaggriculture-multi-route-farming-agent.py` | 110,979 |
| `ref-pipeline-highscore.py` | untested |
| `kaggriculture-precomputed-schedule-policy.py` | **does not parse** (line 36) — needs the extractor extended |

All audited: stdlib only (`base64/copy/json/math/zlib`), no network, no
subprocess, no filesystem writes. Safe to run as sparring partners.

`search/ga_search.py` already rotates one reference agent into the fitness
matchup set each generation.

### Real ladder intelligence
Three real games were downloaded (`kaggle competitions replay <id>`) and
analysed with `analysis/replay_intel.py`. The winners ran **animals with most
of the board empty** — e.g. 7 cows + 3 sheep, zero crops, 56 tiles left empty,
25 tiles never even purchased, beating our 73-tile crop farm 46,563 to 29,118.
Their money curve was *behind* ours until day ~10, then compounded past us.

