## 6. Pitfalls already paid for — do not repeat

1. **`--workers 52` destroys throughput.** 26 physical cores behind 52 logical.
   48 workers took 6+ minutes to not finish one generation; 26 workers do one
   in ~15-30s. Size pools to *physical* cores.
2. **Never edit `agent/main.py` while a search is running.** Workers reload it
   per evaluation, so mid-run edits silently corrupt the fitness signal. Stop
   the search first.
3. **`starter`/`random` are a trap as a fitness signal.** An earlier champion
   won 100% locally against them and scored 485 on the real ladder. Keep the
   reference agents in the matchup set.
4. **Measure at midday, not hour 23.** End-of-day refresh clears `hands` and
   `fed_today`, so an hour-23 sample always reads `hands=0, fed=0` and looks
   like a catastrophic bug when nothing is wrong. Cost me a wrong diagnosis.
5. **Replay tapes only work at their original seed.** Weed spawns and shop
   unlocks are seeded, and — worse — weed RNG is *shared between both farms*
   in one stream, so the opponent's weed pattern depends on how many empty
   tiles **we** have. A recorded route can never be perfectly reproduced.
   `search/make_sparring.py` pins the seed; treat replay opponents as a rough
   benchmark only.
6. **Reference notebooks can execute on import.** `my-2026-08-04-high-score-pipeline`
   wrote `main.py` and `submission.tar.gz` into the CWD when imported. Extract
   payloads with `ast` (as `extract_agents.py` does), don't exec their cells.

### Bugs found in our own agent (all were mine, all were silent)
7. **Goose filler bomb.** Tiles past the end of the priority queue were
   assigned `"GOOSE"`, so any portfolio that didn't fill the whole board
   silently became "...plus ~90 geese" — instant bankruptcy, score ~200.
   This is why the search evolved an all-crop build: over-filling the board
   with crops was the only way to keep the queue from running out. **The
   search was optimising around a bug for 800+ generations / ~5 hours.**
8. **`SELL COW`.** The sell loop iterated the raw shed, which also holds live
   animals. `SELL` of a non-product is a malformed sub-op and the engine
   **aborts the whole order slot**, which killed every milk/wool sale. Only
   ever iterate `SELL_PRIORITY_ORDER`.
9. **Fractional order quantities.** `WHEAT_FEED_BUFFER_MULT` is a searched
   float, so `n - need*mult` produced e.g. `0.485`; the engine `int()`s that
   to 0 and drops the order. All quantities must be whole numbers.
10. **Tiles were ordered from (0,0)** — the farthest corner from the centre
    shed — so the highest-priority roles landed farthest from the shed and
    every daily wheat trip cost 8+ steps. Fixed to nearest-to-shed ordering;
    this alone took the animal build from 253 to 50,481.
11. **Zone thrashing.** `_rebuild_zones` re-cut zones and reset patrol
    pointers whenever the live hand count changed, so units restarted their
    patrol several times a day. Produced nonsense like 8-11 hands scoring 39
    while 6 hands scored 50,000. Fixed by cutting for the day's high-water
    unit count and preserving pointers.
12. **Hiring ignored cash flow.** Hiring to a workload target every day costs
    fib(n) per hand (~2,583/day for 16 hands) and bankrupted us on day 2 from
    a $3,000 start. Fixed with `HIRE_BUDGET_FRACTION` (share of current cash).

### Judgement calls that turned out wrong
13. My first economic analysis concluded *"eggs are the uncapped sink, convert
    everything to geese"*. The two-player shared-market model
    (`portfolio_optimizer.py::two_player_allocate`) and then the real ladder
    both contradicted it — total profit drops to 68% of the single-player
    estimate and animals dominate. Trust the 2P model over the 1P one.
14. I assumed the gap to the references was hand count. It is not — the old
    tile-based formula hired 4 hands and scored 67k, the new workload formula
    hired 11 and scored 54k. **Adding hands to an agent that wastes 60% of
    turns walking just scales the waste.** Fix movement first.

