## 9. Route architecture (2026-08-18) — current line of work

The user chose offline route optimisation. Built in `route/`, which touches
nothing under `agent/` or `search/`, so the old agent and its search still run
unmodified.

### Why it is not a 720-step tape
The reference agents replay a baked `_ACTIONS` tape. We deliberately do not:
weed spawns come from an RNG stream **shared between both farms**, so the
opponent's empty-tile count shifts our weed pattern and no tape is exactly
reproducible (pitfall #5). Instead the *plan* is searched offline and the
*route* is solved fresh each morning from observed state. Same routing win,
no tape fragility. The reference agents need a `_weed_repair_action` for
exactly this reason; we don't.

```
route/geom.py      board geometry. Movement is unrestricted -- LOCKED tiles are
                   passable -- so travel is plain Manhattan, no path search.
route/router.py    the daily routing solver: angular sweep partitions tiles
                   across units packed to each unit's turn budget, then
                   nearest-neighbour + 2-opt orders each unit's stops.
                   Emits a *tour*, not a move tape (a hand's real spawn tile
                   isn't knowable at plan time, so a wrong guess must cost a
                   step, not a desync).
route/agent.py     plan genome + task builder + tour follower + market.
route/evaluate.py  per-episode harness (mirrors search/evaluate.py).
route/search.py    (mu+lambda) over the plan genome. Fitness = mean OWN bank,
                   not margin: we are still behind, and margin against a much
                   stronger fixed opponent is dominated by their score.
```

### Measured progression (seed 11 unless noted)
| | vs starter (5-seed mean) | head-to-head vs v111 |
|---|---|---|
| old `agent/main.py` | ~55k | 26% |
| route, first working run | 82.9k | 40% |
| + idle filler | 113.9k | 38% |
| + front-run & terminal liquidation, filler off | 104.5k | **51%** ($74.9k vs $146.1k) |

Action split went from 25% useful / 60% move / 14% idle to 42% / 43% / 15%.
**Movement is solved** -- 31-43% against the best reference's 43%. Routing is
no longer the bottleneck; sale timing into a contested market is.

### Mechanisms taken from the reference agents
The user confirmed the competition permits using their code directly (all eight
are open-licence, stdlib-only, already audited). Lifted:
- **Front-running the town tick.** Within a step the engine runs
  `_process_market` *before* `_town_consume`, so a sale placed on a step the
  town drains enters an undrained market, while the same sale one step later
  enters after the tick lifted the price. Rule: sell now unless the town
  consumes this item this step. v111 applies it to exactly
  `('MELON','MILK','STRAWBERRY','WOOL')` -- the four steepest glut curves
  (MELON/WOOL quadratic above I0, MILK/STRAWBERRY linear).
- **Terminal liquidation.** Reward is money on hand; shed stock at the buzzer
  is worth zero. Dump everything from `TERMINAL_STEP` (they use 680).

## 10. New pitfalls paid for this session

15. **A crop must be watered on the day it is planted.** `_new_plant` starts a
    plant at `consecutive_unwatered = 1`, so a seedling that isn't watered
    before the nightly refresh hits 2 and is a weed by morning. Planning PLANT
    and WATER as separate days silently killed all 73 crops we planted; the
    whole crop portfolio produced nothing. Fixing it alone took seed 11 from
    $29,202 to $90,201. PLANT and WATER must ride in the same tile visit.
16. **`starter` and head-to-head can move in *opposite* directions.** The idle
    filler was worth +37% vs starter and **-4pp** head-to-head, because the
    extra marginal product lands in a market the reference has already crashed.
    Flooding is a spoiling move that costs us more than it costs them. Never
    accept a change on its vs-starter number alone -- this is pitfall #3 with
    a live example.
17. **`pkill -f <pattern>` matches your own shell.** `pkill -f ga_search` from
    a bash -c whose command line contains "ga_search" kills the shell running
    it. Use a bracket class: `pkill -f '[g]a_search'`.
18. **Editing `route/agent.py` mid-search corrupts fitness**, same as pitfall #2
    -- `route/evaluate.py` re-execs the file per episode. Stop the search first.

## 11. Opponent modelling (`route/opponent.py`)

Two independent signals, kept separate because they fail differently.

**Behavioural — the opponent's sales are EXACTLY recoverable, not guessed.**
Within a step the engine applies both players' market orders and then the
town's consumption, so

    inv[t+1] = inv[t] + my_sales[t] + their_sales[t] - town_take[t]

and every term but `their_sales` is known: inventory is public, `town_take` is
computable from the unlocked shop list, and our own sales are what we issued.
Validated against engine ground truth over a full episode:

| item | inferred | true | err |
|---|---|---|---|
| STRAWBERRY / MELON / MILK / WOOL / EGG | 278 / 102 / 215 / 94 / 0 | identical | **0** |
| WHEAT | 237 | 244 | -7 |
| FERTILIZER | 215 | 217 | -2 |

Total absolute error 9 for a whole season, entirely in WHEAT and FERTILIZER --
the only two products whose price reaches the $1 floor, where the engine
deliberately does *not* add the sale to inventory. Exact on all four front-run
products.

**Structural** — their farm is public, so `forecast_supply` projects their
visible tiles forward with the engine's own yield rules (blind to their shed).
`classify()` reads style/tempo; it correctly labels v111 `animal-heavy,
{COW: 8, SHEEP: 4}`.

`pressure()` fuses them into supply-vs-remaining-town-demand; `reserve_scale()`
turns that into a reserve-price multiplier, and a predicted glut also cancels
the front-run hold (no point waiting a step if they are about to bury the
market). Worth ~+$1.1k/game head-to-head, consistent in sign across 4 seeds.

## 12. Measurement pitfalls (these produced two wrong conclusions)

19. **Never attribute engine callbacks by comparing against objects from `obs`.**
    `kaggle_environments` structifies/copies the observation before the agent
    sees it, so `farm is obs["farms"][0]` is *always false* inside a patched
    `_commit_unit`, and every commit gets attributed to one player. This
    produced "we sold 0 of everything" while banking $58k. Hook
    `_process_market(state, env)` instead -- it receives the live state -- and
    identify players by `private` identity.
20. **A validation whose "ground truth" shares the broken step with the thing
    being validated proves nothing.** The opponent-sales inference first
    "validated" at near-zero error only because both sides of the comparison
    were computing *combined* sales. Re-run with correct attribution it really
    is exact, but the first test was circular and would have passed for a
    completely broken model.
21. **v111 does *not* profit from us.** An earlier note here compared v111's
    121k-vs-starter to 146k-vs-us and concluded we were feeding it. Those were
    measured in different seats. Same seed, v111 in seat 0 throughout:
    starter 195,365 / pass 190,184 / random 180,831 / **our agent 146,063**.
    We suppress it more than any other opponent. Also: the unlocked *shop mix*
    changes with the opponent (weeds only roll on empty tiles, shifting the RNG
    before `rng.choice(SHOPS)`), so cross-opponent score comparisons are
    confounded and cannot support strategy claims at all.

## 13. Pivot to the reference lineage (2026-08-18, later)

`route/tournament.py` runs a round-robin over every loadable reference agent,
both seat orders (seat is not neutral -- market orders settle in player order
and player 0's weeds roll first). First run, 126 games:

| agent | win% |
|---|---|
| multi-route-farming-agent | 72% |
| 3000-socre / ttv1 | 69% |
| rank-your-agent | 64% |
| **v111-8c4s** | **47%** |
| ref-pipeline-highscore | 17% |
| **our route agent** | **0% (0/36)** |

Two corrections this forced:
- **v111 was the wrong benchmark.** Everything in sections 9-12 was measured
  against the 5th-strongest agent.
- Our own architecture lost every game. Sections 9-12 remain accurate as
  measurements, but the runtime-routing line was abandoned here.

### What the strongest agent actually does
`multi-route` carries **five** precomputed tapes and picks one at runtime from
the town's shop draw: `6c12s_4q_first_yarn` when a YARN_STORE opens first (a
single-product shop eats 2 wool/tick), `10c4s_3q` when the milk shops appear,
`8c6s_3q` otherwise. That is the mechanism we never had -- our portfolio was
fixed at day 0 and ignored the shops entirely, which is why our wool realised
$19.8/unit against their $61.

### The win: their constants were never optimised
The tapes are optimal and untouched. The runtime layer wrapped around them is
hand-set scalars. `route/batch.py` scores N variants on one fixed matchup set;
two rounds of 12 and 10 variants found:

    _PREEMPT_MIN_FUTURE_QUANTITY: 4 -> 0     (the load-bearing one)
    _PREEMPT_MAX_BATCH:          12 -> 30

280-game round-robin: **TUNED 77%** vs the published agent's **60%**, +17pp,
and it tops the whole field. Baked by `route/bake.py` into
`submission/main.py` (148KB, stdlib-only, verified importable with the project
off sys.path).

**Score by wins, not mean bank.** All 12 first-round variants sat within $133
of each other on mean money while win rate ranged 33-100% -- the tape dominates
the bank, and the runtime layer only decides close games. Ranking by mean money
picked the wrong variant.

## 14. PBT as specified is a random walk — sample size, not strategy

Ran the population-based-training protocol (`pbt/`) for 10 rounds. It produced a
champion that then lost **22%-78% over 50 games to the variant the earlier
manual batch rounds had already found**. Ten rounds of "optimisation" moved
backwards.

The cause is selection sample size, and it generalises to any search here:

- Real differences between runtime-constant variants are **1-3 win-rate points**.
  Resolving that needs hundreds of games; the protocol scored each variant on
  **12**.
- With 12 games the ranking is dominated by seed noise, so `v8 noise: small
  gaussian on every constant` kept "winning" and the genome random-walked away
  from the good setting.
- The stop condition ("champion sweeps the pool, 5 rounds running") fired twice
  spuriously. First because sampling 3 opponents from a pool containing a 0%
  agent makes rank-1 nearly free; then, after hardening it to a full-pool
  gauntlet, because 4 seeds cannot separate near-identical agents (the pool
  fills with past champions that share the same tape).

Measured truth over 50 games each, both seats:

| matchup | win rate |
|---|---|
| SUB_v12 vs published base | **62%** |
| AGGRO_v11 vs published base | 58% |
| PBT round-10 champion vs base | 54% |
| PBT round-10 champion vs SUB_v12 | **22%** |

**Rule for any future search on this agent: a variant needs >= 100 games before
its rank means anything.** Mean bank is useless as a selection signal (all
variants sit within ~$150 of each other; the tape sets the bank). Rank by wins,
over enough games.

Current best remains `_PREEMPT_MIN_FUTURE_QUANTITY=0`, `_PREEMPT_MAX_BATCH=30`
on the multi-route base -- what is already in `submission/main.py`.

## 15. The base agent IS the first-place player's agent

`multi-route-farming-agent` is カワシギ's. Its own function names give it away --
`_kawa_route_label`, `_kawa_actions`, `_KAWA_MILK_SUPPORT` -- and its step-0
market order matches the published description of that opening exactly:

    [HIRE x5, BUY_ANIMAL COW 2, BUY_ANIMAL SHEEP 2,
     BUY_SEED WHEAT 7, BUY_SEED MELON 12, BUY_PRODUCT WHEAT 6]

So it is already in the pool as `ref_A`, it is the base our submission is built
on, and the tuned build beats it.

**A circulating architectural write-up of that agent is wrong** and would send
work in the wrong direction if believed: it credits an A* pathfinder, a central
job queue with mutex tile locks, and a runtime ROI calculator. The source has
none of those. It replays five precomputed 720-step tapes and picks one from the
town's shop draw. The "fluid micro" visible in a replay was solved offline.

## 16. Counter-parameters per opponent: the premise does not hold

Measured a 6-config x 6-opponent grid (`pbt/counter.py`, writes
`models/counter_params.json` and `opponent_features.csv`):

| config | ref_A | ref_B | ref_C | ref_D | ref_E | ref_F |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| base | 38% | 100% | 50% | 100% | 50% | 100% |
| sub_v12 | 75% | 100% | 50% | 100% | 50% | 100% |
| aggro | 88% | 100% | 50% | 100% | 50% | 100% |
| preempt off | 12% | 100% | 50% | 100% | 50% | 100% |

Only **ref_A** responds to the constants at all. ref_B/D/F are already 100%
losses for them under every config, and ref_C/E sit at a dead 50% no matter what
-- both are tape agents whose farms barely interact with ours, so the games are
decided by the seed, not by play. Per-family counter-parameters and K-Means
clustering therefore have nothing to separate: the whole problem is "beat ref_A".

Corollary: any headline win-rate over this field is mostly fixed. Moving ref_A
from 38% to 88% moves the overall number by ~8 points and everything else is
saturated.

## 17. Submission confirmed, and win rates here are NON-TRANSITIVE

100 games per matchup (50 seeds x both seats), 95% CIs:

| matchup | win rate | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| **sub_v12 vs base** | **70%** | [61%, 79%] |
| combo vs base | 70% | [61%, 79%] |
| aggro vs base | 53% | [43%, 63%] |
| aggro vs sub_v12 | 32% | [23%, 41%] |
| combo vs sub_v12 | **17%** | [10%, 24%] |
| combo vs aggro | 68% | [59%, 77%] |

`sub_v12` (`_PREEMPT_MIN_FUTURE_QUANTITY=0`, `_PREEMPT_MAX_BATCH=30`) is the
build in `submission/main.py` and is confirmed best.

Two traps recorded:

- The 8-game counter grid in section 16 ranked `aggro` top (88% vs ref_A). At
  100 games it is 53% [43%, 63%] vs base -- indistinguishable from the untuned
  agent. Third time this session a small sample gave a confidently wrong answer
  (see also section 14). **>= 100 games, always.**
- **Win rate is not transitive here.** `combo` beats base 70% and beats aggro
  68%, yet loses to sub_v12 17-83. A single "overall win%" league table conceals
  this; matchups have to be read pairwise. Ladder standing therefore depends on
  who is actually on the ladder, not on our field-average.

## 18. Ladder feedback: 1887, and why the day-1 gap cannot be patched

First real signal: **1887** on the ladder. Losses traced to a defect that is
baked into **all five tapes**: day 1 has 0 hand slots, 0 hand actions and 0 HIRE
orders, so the farm runs that whole day on the farmer alone.

    day 0: money $3,000  hires 5  hands 5  hand-actions 89
    day 1: money $23     hires 0  hands 0  hand-actions 0   <-- dead day
    day 2: money $176    hires 4  hands 4  hand-actions 59

Not a cash problem: four hires cost fib(0..3) = $7 against $23 on hand.

**It is nevertheless unpatchable from outside the tape.** Measured, 40 games vs
ref_A each:

| overlay | win rate vs ref_A |
|---|---|
| none (submission) | **62%** |
| hire on day 1 only, no work assigned | 62% (bodies stand idle -- tape has no actions for them) |
| hire every day | **0%** |
| hire + assign work, day 1 only | **5%** |
| hire + assign work, every day | **0%** |

Two independent mechanisms, both fatal:

1. **Hiring ahead of the tape reprices its own hires.** Cost is
   `fib(hires_today)`; taking four first moves the tape's five from $7 to
   fib(4..8) = $81, which it cannot pay at $23, so it goes crewless.
2. **The tape assumes an exact state trajectory.** Watering one extra plant or
   harvesting one animal early on day 1 makes later scripted actions miss --
   HARVEST on an empty tile, FEED on a fed animal, SELL of stock that is not
   there. A tape `PASS` is a hand holding station, not an idle hand.

So the free day of labour is genuinely unreachable without re-optimising the
tape itself. That is the case for building our own offline schedule optimiser --
the same conclusion section 9 reached from the other direction.

## 19. The submission is not exploitable, and league tables lie here

Swept 50 constant configurations against the submission, 40 games each: **none
beat it.** Best adversary is the untuned base at 38%; worst 20%. Within this
parameter family sub_v12 is dominant, so an opponent holding our code cannot
tune a counter to it.

The v2 adaptive agent (`pbt/adaptive.py`, runtime opponent identification at day
4 -> per-family counter constants) was built and entered: it finished **exactly
tied with sub_v12** (86/144 wins, identical mean bank), because the measured
counter table selects sub_v12 for every family. K-Means was dropped as
unnecessary -- six opponents separate exactly at day 4 on raw counts.

**Do not pick a submission from the league table.** In the 720-game table
`aggro` places first at 65% and sub_v12 third at 60%, yet head-to-head over 100
games aggro loses to sub_v12 **32-68**. Win rate here is non-transitive; rank
pairwise against the agent you actually expect to meet.

## 20. Opponent modelling that finally paid: market intervention (+10pp)

Of the counter-parameter list, only the market lever is reachable. Opening hire
count, feed volume, quadrant timing and crop preference are compiled into the
tape and cannot be moved from outside (section 18). Market orders change no
tile, so they are safe to add.

`pbt/intervene.py` bakes an overlay that times sales off the exact
opponent-sales inference: it measures their per-item sale cadence from public
market inventory, and one turn before the predicted sale pushes stock into the
book so they quote into a depressed market.

Validated at 100 games per matchup:

| matchup | win rate | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| dump 40% vs ref_A | **80%** | [72%, 88%] |
| dump 60% vs ref_A | 80% | [72%, 88%] |
| dump 80% vs ref_A | 79% | [71%, 87%] |
| previous submission vs ref_A | 70% | [61%, 79%] |
| **dump 40% vs previous submission** | **85%** | [78%, 92%] |
| dump 80% vs ref_C / ref_B | 76% / 88% | unchanged from before |

The **wheat squeeze** in the same spec (buy feed inside an animal-heavy
opponent's window to lift their price) is catastrophic: **2%**. It spends cash
the tape needs downstream. Dropped.

Supporting modules built: `pbt/features.py` (the six-dim vector; five come off
the public farm, `market_tx_frequency` off the exact inference) and
`pbt/cluster.py` (K-Means K=5, k-means++ seeded, z-scored -- written out because
the venv has no sklearn and `cash_burn_rate_day5` would otherwise be the only
dimension the distance sees).

## 21. A packaging bug that would have scored ZERO

Kaggle resolves a file agent with `get_last_callable`, which walks the module
namespace in **insertion order**. Rebinding `agent` in an appended overlay does
**not** move it -- the name was inserted by the base file near line 988 -- so the
last *newly defined* callable wins. With the intervention overlay appended that
was `_iv_predict`, and the engine called it as
`_iv_predict(observation, configuration)`:

    TypeError: cannot use Struct as a dict key (unhashable type: 'Struct')

Every in-process test passed, because those call `module.agent` directly. Only
loading the file **by path**, the way Kaggle does, exposes it.

`route/bake.py` now always appends a final `_submission_entry(obs)` stub, and it
must remain the last definition in the file. **Any future overlay must be
validated by path (`env.run([path, opponent])`), never by import.**

## 22. V16-RC5 is v111 (identical SHA-256) -- do not submit it

The V16-RC5 notebook's `main.py` is byte-for-byte
`opponents/v111-8c4s-economic-core-premium-lead.py`:

    sha256 f029fa0cb66a9eb509afbe44e3f59b800332d0419db91607183410e4089c4d19
    18,946 bytes -- matches the notebook's own asserted hash and size

Its headline results ("60/60", "+18,992 mean margin") are measured against its
own reconstructed core and three public artifacts we do not hold. It was never
run against kawa/multi-route. In our 720-game round-robin v111 places **5th at
47%**, behind multi-route (55%) and well behind our build. Submitting it is a
downgrade.

The **Market Relay** described alongside that notebook is *not* in that file --
the shipped code front-runs one turn on four premium items with no gate. The
described mechanism (3-turn lead, FERTILIZER included, exact repayment,
near-mirror gate) is a later version, and its four ideas were tested separately:

| relay variant | vs ref_A | overall (120 games) |
|---|---|---|
| **lead 3 + FERTILIZER** | **82%** | 80% |
| lead 3 | 82% | 80% |
| lead 1 + FERTILIZER | 78% | 80% |
| lead 1 (previous submission) | 78% | 80% |
| near-mirror gate | 72% | 78% |
| exact repayment, lead 1 | 68% | 77% |
| exact repayment, lead 3 | 68% | **75%** |

**Two of the four ideas are actively harmful here:**

- **Exact repayment hurts** (75-77% vs 80%). Conserving the two-turn volume is
  right for an agent whose sales are already optimally sized; ours deliberately
  dumps 40% of held stock as an aggressive act, and repaying hands the advantage
  straight back.
- **Near-mirror gating hurts** (78%). It only reduces how often we act, and
  every opponent in this field is worth acting against.

Adopted: **lead 3 + FERTILIZER**. Decisive 100-game pairwise:

| matchup | win rate | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| lead3+fert vs previous submission | **88%** | [82%, 94%] |
| lead3+fert vs ref_A | **87%** | [80%, 94%] |
| previous submission vs ref_A | 80% | [72%, 88%] |

Note the screening panel scored all four leading variants at an identical 80%
overall while the direct 100-game pairing separated them cleanly at 88%.
**Screen broadly, decide pairwise.**

## 23. Tape selection is already optimal -- the expansion/crop/hiring search is exhausted

The parameter-search protocol targets expansion rhythm, crop mix and hiring
schedule. None of the three is editable on this architecture -- all are compiled
into the tapes, and any overlay touching farm state or pre-empting a HIRE
measures 0-5% (section 18).

The reachable form of that same search is the **tape-selection rule**: the base
agent ships five tapes and each one *is* a distinct mix and expansion plan
(`10c4s_3q`, `8c6s_3q`, `6c8s_3q`, `6c12s_4q_first_yarn`,
`6c12s_4q_second_yarn`). `_kawa_route_label` maps the town's shop draw onto them
through five hand-written buckets, and that mapping had never been searched.
`pbt/tapesel.py` makes it searchable; nothing touches a tile.

Bucket firing frequency, measured over 120 games:

| bucket | condition | fires |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | YARN_STORE first | 18% |
| 1 | YARN_STORE in first two | 5% |
| 2 | YARN_STORE in first three | 7% |
| **3** | **milk shop in first three** | **57%** |
| 4 | otherwise | 13% |

Coordinate scan, all 5 tapes in each of the 5 buckets, 120 games per config:

- **Every alternative for bucket 3 -- the one that fires 57% of the time --
  ranked below the default.** The hand-written `10c4s_3q` choice is optimal.
- The only configs scoring above default were +1% (one game in 120) and both sat
  in low-frequency buckets.

Decisive 100-game pairwise on those two "+1%" changes combined:

| matchup | win rate | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| combined change vs default mapping | **26%** | [17%, 35%] |
| combined change vs kawa | 87% | [80%, 94%] |
| default mapping vs kawa | 87% | [80%, 94%] |

The apparent gain was not merely noise -- it was **inverted**. Third occurrence
of the same pattern: a screening panel rates variants equal or slightly better,
and the direct pairing shows one clearly worse. Screening panels rank against a
*field*; only pairwise ranks against an *opponent*, and this game is
non-transitive.

**Conclusion: this dimension is exhausted.** The submission is unchanged.

## 24. SEAT ASYMMETRY -- win rate is the wrong metric for same-tape matchups

**An agent playing itself wins seat 0 only 6/40 = 15%.** Identical file, both
seats, 40 seeds. Mean money margin: **-$66** on a ~$90k bank. kawa against
itself gives exactly the same figures.

Same-tape matchups are therefore effective *ties* whose winner is decided by
structural seat asymmetry -- `_end_of_day` rolls player 0's weeds first, and
`_process_market` resolves atomic HIRE/BUY_LAND in player order -- not by
strategy. Every sibling-vs-sibling win rate reported earlier in this document
(e.g. "88% vs sub_v12") measures that coin flip, not skill.

**Use paired margin instead.** For each seed play both seat orders and sum the
two margins; the seat term cancels exactly. Validated: an agent against a
byte-identical copy of itself scores **+0 mean, +0 median, 0 paired wins, 0
paired losses**. Win rate on the same data would have said 15% or 85% depending
on which side you counted.

Numbers against *structurally different* opponents were never in doubt -- those
margins are large ($1.2k-$15k) and survive either metric.

## 25. We were exploitable, and turning our own weapon around found the fix

`_future_sells(obs, step)` in kawa reads **its own tape**, not us -- the `obs`
argument only selects which tape. So kawa does not model our sales. But
`_clone_distance(obs) > _PREEMPT_MAX_CLONE_DISTANCE` does read our farm, and our
measured clone distance against kawa is **0** (we run the same tape), so its
preemption is fully armed against us. Denying it is worth **+5pp / +$784**
(87% -> 92%, +$665 -> +$1,449 when kawa runs with `_PREEMPT_ENABLED=False`).

That prize is unreachable: raising the distance past 6 requires 7 extra hands
(fib-priced, ruinous), 7 changed tiles (breaks the tape, 0-5%), or a different
tape (measured net-negative in section 23). The signature is derived from farm
state the tape owns.

The second-order question -- *what if the opponent models us?* -- did pay off.
The earlier adversary sweep (section 19) was incomplete: it varied preempt
constants but **never equipped an opponent with our own market-intervention
layer**. Doing so produces a predator that infers our sales exactly as we infer
theirs, and three of them beat us:

| predator | paired margin vs us | paired W/L |
|---|---|---|
| dump 80%, lead 3, +fert | **+467** | **39/1** |
| dump 80%, lead 5, +fert | +336 | 34/5 |
| dump 40%, lead 5, +fert | +283 | 36/4 |
| byte-identical copy | +0 | 0/0 |

So our own dump fraction was simply too low. Sweeping it by paired margin:

| dump | vs kawa | mirror vs d40 |
|---|---|---|
| 40% (previous) | +1,221 | -- |
| 60% | +1,358 | +297 (37/3) |
| **80%** | **+1,428** | **+467 (39/1)** |
| 100% | +1,209 | +29 (29/11) |

Adopted 80%. Against the wider field all fractions sit within ~$26 of each other
(noise), so the mirror matchup is what decides it -- and on a ladder where most
entries descend from the same public notebooks, mirror matchups are common.

## 26. RETRACTION: the constant tuning was worth nothing

Sections 13 and 19 claimed `_PREEMPT_MIN_FUTURE_QUANTITY=0` + `_PREEMPT_MAX_BATCH=30`
beat the published agent by +17pp (77% vs 60%). **That was the seat coin flip,
not strategy.** Re-measured by paired margin over a 3,120-game round-robin:

| model | vs field | vs kawa |
|---|---|---|
| **d80 lead-3 +fert (current)** | **+9,016** | **+1,415** |
| d40 lead-3 +fert | +8,912 | +1,212 |
| d40 lead-1 | +8,332 | +464 |
| aggro | +8,185 | +89 |
| PBT_r5 | +8,119 | **-7** |
| kawa / base_untuned | +8,103 | 0 |
| **sub_v12 (the "+17pp" build)** | +8,096 | **-7** |
| ref_B / ref_D | +1,608 | -9,574 |
| ref_E v111 | -13,338 | -18,621 |
| ref_C | -13,624 | -18,752 |
| ref_F | -43,120 | -43,877 |

sub_v12, PBT_r5 and aggro are all statistically **tied with the untuned base**.
Every generation of constant search in sections 13-23 produced nothing. The
entire real edge comes from the market-intervention layer: +464 -> +1,212 ->
+1,415 as lead, fertilizer and dump fraction were added.

And that edge is thin: **+$1,415 on a ~$90,000 bank is 1.6%.** It wins
consistently (0/40 paired losses to kawa) but leaves little room on a ladder
whose opponents differ from this field. Ladder scores of 1887 then 1800 are
consistent with submissions that were, in substance, kawa.

## 27. When the opponent model needs to run

Observation is **every turn** and cannot be sampled: the inference is a running
inventory delta, so a skipped turn breaks the arithmetic.

The *action* is sparse -- measured over one full episode against kawa:

- dumps fire **65 times in 720 turns = 9%**
- never before **day 6** (needs >=3 observations to estimate a sale period)
- concentrated in days 23-27, where volumes are largest
- by product: FERTILIZER 33, STRAWBERRY 17, MILK 9, WOOL 4, MELON 2

FERTILIZER alone is half of all firings -- the item added from the Market Relay
write-up, and the single most valuable part of that idea.

## 28. Ladder replays: both user submissions are plain kawa

`kaggle competitions replay <EPISODE_ID>` works from this machine, and
`pbt/extract_tape.py` pulls any player's 719-step tape out of a replay and
transplants it into kawa's runtime scaffolding (weed repair + hand alignment).
Faithfulness verified: replaying both extracted tapes at the episode's real seed
reproduced 107,064 v 135,395 against an actual 108,217 v 135,557.

**Note the off-by-one:** replay `steps[0]` is the initial state and carries no
action. Actions live at `steps[1:]`, which is why the reference tapes are 719
long. Taking `steps[0]` silently discards the opening turn.

Extracting our own play from submission 55594505 (the 2326.3 one) and comparing
against kawa's five tapes:

| kawa tape | farmer actions matched | market matched |
|---|---|---|
| **6c12s_4q_first_yarn** | **100.0%** | 90.1% |
| 8c6s_3q / 10c4s_3q | 65.2% | 52.7% |
| 6c12s_4q_second_yarn | 61.2% | 49.0% |

**Both submissions are unmodified kawa.** The 2326.3 vs 1898.5 gap is rating
convergence and match history, not a difference in agent. There is no secret
stronger file to recover.

### The ladder meta has converged on 8c4s
Three different opponents (Naru041104, Igor V, StopPlantingStartGameTheorying)
open with the same line -- 4-5 HIRE, **1 COW / 4 SHEEP**, 5 wheat seed, 5 melon
seed, 5-14 wheat product -- and all reach **14 hands**. kawa's tapes allocate
only **12 hand slots** (277 HIRE commands but 12 slots), giving ~22% less labour
throughput: 2,741 useful actions against Naru's 3,494.

Yet over 40 paired seeds kawa still beats v111 by +12,232 and our build beats
Naru's extracted tape by +8,860 (29/40). The three losses shown were a bad draw
from a ~30% loss rate, not a systematic defeat.

### Seed distribution is NOT the problem (hypothesis tested and rejected)
Ladder seeds are 32-bit (681050694, 2014467720, 1623205117) while all local work
used 1-50. Tested 40 small vs 40 large seeds:

| matchup | seeds 1-40 | 32-bit seeds |
|---|---|---|
| ours vs naru_tape | +11,374 (28/40) | +8,860 (29/40) |
| kawa vs v111 | +13,862 (31/40) | +12,232 (32/40) |
| ours vs kawa | +1,428 (40/40) | +1,611 (39/40) |

The populations agree. A single-seed sign flip was one of the ~11 losing draws,
not evidence of bias.

### Why the +1,611 matters
Real ladder wins by the current submission were **+659, +1,825, +3,350**. Our
measured improvement over plain kawa (+1,611, winning 39 of 40 paired seeds) is
the same order as the margins that actually decide these games.
